' bug-467: writing to a socket whose peer has gone away must RAISE, and the
' process must survive to run the TRAP.
'
' Before the fix this fixture could not exist: the second `tcp::write` after the
' peer's close was delivered SIGPIPE, whose default disposition terminates, so
' the process died with signal 13 (exit 141) before `write` returned `EPIPE`.
' The TRAP never ran, the scope drops never ran, and `main` never returned --
' which made this a remote denial of service, since a server's peer is untrusted
' input and any client that connects and immediately disconnects ended the whole
' server. The program entry now installs `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN)` on every
' POSIX target, so the write fails with `EPIPE` and `tcp/gen_io.rs`'s existing
' errno classification names it `ErrConnectionClosed`.
'
' The loop writes MANY times, and each write is LARGE, both on purpose. A single
' small write after the peer's close SUCCEEDS -- it is accepted by the local
' stack, and only the peer's RST makes a later one fail -- so a one-write probe
' passes against the broken compiler and proves nothing. See the comment on the
' chunk below for why the size is what makes this a stable golden.
'
' This fixture's job is to be DETERMINISTIC, so it is the shape that always
' reports the raise. Catching a regression is the job of
' `tests/rt_sigpipe_socket_and_pipe.rs`, which runs the small-write shape
' repeatedly and asserts that no run is ever killed by a signal -- that shape is
' likelier to take SIGPIPE per run but cannot be pinned by a golden.
'
' The mirror of `rt-behavior/tcp/tcp-read-eof-raises-rt`: both directions of a
' departed peer now report the same `ErrConnectionClosed`, and `tls` agrees.
IMPORT errorCode
IMPORT io
IMPORT net
IMPORT strings
IMPORT tcp

' Connect a loopback pair, close the client outright (not a shutdown -- the
' socket goes away, so data arriving for it is answered with an RST), then write
' until the failure surfaces.
FUNC writeAfterPeerClose AS String
  RES server = tcp::listen("127.0.0.1", 0)
  LET bound = tcp::localAddress(server)
  RES client = tcp::connect("127.0.0.1", bound.port)
  RES conn = tcp::accept(server)
  tcp::close(client)
  ' The chunk is 64 KiB, not a few bytes, and that is what makes this fixture a
  ' STABLE golden. Small writes are all accepted by the local send buffer, so
  ' whether any of them fails depends on the peer's RST racing the loop: with
  ' 32-byte writes this fixture printed `raised=TRUE` on an idle machine but
  ' `completed=TRUE` under the load of a full acceptance sweep, i.e. it went red
  ' on a CORRECT build. Chunks this size fill the send buffer within a few
  ' iterations, so the write BLOCKS waiting for ACKs that a departed peer will
  ' never send -- and a blocked write is exactly where the failure surfaces.
  ' Measured on the fixed build: 10/10 raised idle, 20/20 raised under eight
  ' spinning CPU hogs.
  '
  ' Which iteration raises still varies (1-4 across those runs), so the output
  ' reports only THAT it raised and with which code, never a count.
  LET chunk AS String = strings::repeat("x", 65536)
  FOR i = 1 TO 200
    tcp::write(conn, chunk)
  NEXT
  RETURN "completed=TRUE"
  TRAP(err)
    RETURN "raised=" & toString(err.code = errorCode::ErrConnectionClosed)
  END TRAP
END FUNC

' An empty write is a no-op that never reaches a syscall, so it stays successful
' even when the peer is gone -- pinned here so the fix above cannot be "improved"
' into raising on a call that sends nothing.
FUNC emptyWriteAfterPeerClose AS String
  RES server = tcp::listen("127.0.0.1", 0)
  LET bound = tcp::localAddress(server)
  RES client = tcp::connect("127.0.0.1", bound.port)
  RES conn = tcp::accept(server)
  tcp::close(client)
  LET noBytes AS List OF Byte = []
  tcp::write(conn, noBytes)
  tcp::write(conn, "")
  RETURN "emptyWritesSucceeded=TRUE"
  TRAP(err)
    RETURN "emptyWriteRaised=" & toString(err.code)
  END TRAP
END FUNC

FUNC main AS Integer
  io::print("write " & writeAfterPeerClose())
  io::print("empty " & emptyWriteAfterPeerClose())
  RETURN 0
END FUNC
